welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Professor John Romer the Elizabeth s  and a very stout professor of political  science and economics professor roamers  research concerns political economy and  distributive justice his books include  racism xenophobia and distribution  democracy education and equality  political competition equality of  opportunity and theories of distributive  justice professor Romer is here today to  talk about the future of capitalism  welcome professor Romer Thank You Harlan  in your most recent work you explore the  future of capitalism give us your  perspective well because we're in this  massive crisis a lot of people are  talking about what should be done to  prevent these crises from happening in  the future as well as obviously solving  the present crisis but I think there's  been a wrongheaded approach to the  question of how to prevent these crises  in the future in the following sense  that all the discussants in this debate  or almost all of them take people as  they are including people on Wall Street  and truck and are trying to design  techniques to make sure that a system  which is run by people who a financial  sector that's run in large part by  people who are out to maximize their  wealth and our greedy that will work  even under such a system and I think the  history of capitalism shows that there's  really no way of designing a foolproof  set of rules that will work if there are  intelligent and avaricious people who  want to circumvent them ok so how do the  preferences of individuals fit into your  theory so there's been a  there's been a very active area of  research in economics over the last 30  years which attempts to design rules  which will lead people regardless of  what their values or preferences are to  do things which are in socially optimal  good for the welfare of people  the simplest example with which most  people are familiar is the Adam Smith  quote that if even though the baker and  the candlestick maker and so on are out  for their own good and not to maximize  their own profits in a market system  with competition if they do so that will  lead to the social good well there's  been a lot of criticism of that naive  less a fair philosophy in in the last  hundred years at least and but the in  the last thirty years as I said there's  been an effort in economics to see if  they can if we can design more  sophisticated systems of rules that will  work even when people are greedy and  avaricious and are only looking out for  themselves work in the sense of  delivering outcomes that are good for  society and in fact the Nobel Prize was  awarded to three economists two years  ago who were instrumental in developing  this this area my view however is that  it's really not going to be possible to  design such rules given people who are  avaricious and out only for themselves  that the only hope of designing rules  which will bring about a good outcome in  society and in a market economy is if  people's preferences change and become  more concerned with other people more  solid heuristic you argue that crises  can bring about greater social insurance  what do you mean by that  so how would preferences change I I'm  not a utopian in the sense that I don't  believe that  jawboning by  even as charismatic a politician as  Barack Obama or other leaders or more  ethics courses and business schools is  going to be enough to get people to  think more solid heuristically I think  however that there are laws of motion of  the development of solidarity and my own  view is that crises can start off a  sequence of events that may end up in  people's becoming more solid heuristic  and changing their values and their  preferences do you think that it's  possible for capitalism to achieve  stability and relative equality of  condition  so let me let me before I get to that  let me explain this sequence of events  so one thing that crises do is they tend  to reduce all people to the same  situation they wipe out wealth they in  particular they make the risks that  different people face much more similar  than they were before the crisis so  middle class people let's say and  working-class people now are facing  risks which are much more similar with  respect to unemployment and the  possibility of financing their  retirement than was the case before the  crisis and when people face risks that  when people have risk exposures which  are very similar in terms of the chances  of good and bad things happening to them  it's much easier to design universal  insurance everybody will want the  insurance and so in particular I think  that one good consequence of this  current crisis every cloud has a silver  lining is that national health insurance  will be much easier to pass now because  there'll be much more universal support  for it I think once these insurance  insurance is extended in this manner  then I think people's preferences change  because they like they begin to like  the society where a certain kind of  poverty is eliminated there won't be  people without insurance there won't be  crowded emergency rooms full of  uninsured poor people who can't get care  any other way and even costs will  probably go down because or they won't  accelerate so fast because with  universal insurance the the government  will have a an incentive to keep prices  down and for instance drug prices so it  seems to me that what will happen is  after the passage of health insurance  people will come to like that new kind  of society just the way people came to  like the system with Social Security  after 1935 despite the fact that the  right wing had opposed Social Security  very strenuously as they are now or have  been opposing national health insurance  in the past 20 years so my view is that  insurance which is initially adopted  because from a self-interested viewpoint  people are facing the same risks and so  they want the everybody wants the  insurance then people begin to like the  new society and their preferences become  somewhat more desiring of equality and  then they go farther they pass new kinds  of social insurance because their  preferences have changed and they want  to get rid of other kinds of poverty and  other kinds of inequality so I think a  crisis can set in motion sequence of  insurance followed by preference changes  followed by more insurance which leads  to more quality and I think there are  two major historical examples of this in  recent history one is the Great  Depression which made people so similar  in their risk profiles that there was  support for Social Security and the  other was the Second World War which  wiped out a lot of wealth in Europe and  in Japan and homogenized people's risk  exposures in that sense and the  consequence of the second world war was  a massive growth in the welfare state  after the Second World War that is to  say many more insurance programs were  voted in by citizens as a consequence I  think  of the crisis of the war okay you cite  the Scandinavian experience as  particularly successful do you think the  US can learn something from them so I  think Scandinavia is often cited as a  special case because they these are  small countries the Nordic countries and  they're at least in the time when they  enacted their the legislation which  brought about their welfare states there  were very homogeneous countries with  respect to ethnic city and language and  religion and so many people say that  there was a natural solidarity among  Scandinavians because of theirs their  racial and similarities and religious  homogeneity and so on and so they had a  natural feeling of solidarity for each  other and that's when engendered the  large welfare states I actually  challenge that view I think that this  that the homogeneity of Scandinavia was  important because it also meant that the  people were very similar with respect to  the risks they faced with respect to  illness and with respect to unemployment  and wit and longevity so the and I think  it was that homogeneity of the risk  exposures which made it easy for the  welfare state to get started in these  countries and then the process that I  mentioned I think took over and the  Scandinavian countries have gone much  farther than simple self-interested  insurance would require now as far as  the United States is concerned you said  what's the lesson for the u.s. well the  parallel I'm drawing is that we're now  in a crisis which is going to homogenize  people with respect to their risk  exposures at least the working class in  the middle class to some extent and I  think that will form a powerful  electoral block or support for social  insurance which will then change  people's preferences the difference of  course between the United States and  Scandinavia is that we have a very large  minority population we're a very  heterogeneous country ethnically and I  think the racism that has been the  result of slavery  is the main reason that there hasn't  been a larger welfare state in the  United States and until now so it's been  slow going here because of the history  of slavery and the lack of solidarity  that that brought about between between  white and black citizens okay so what do  you think is the future of capitalism  here in this country it's very hard to  say I mean I don't I don't have I don't  have any crystal ball and I think there  are a lot of people who are much more  knowledgeable about the financial sector  than I am a lot of economists like Joe  Stiglitz and Paul Krugman and so on  and I look to them for instruction about  the details of what's going to happen  but my hope is as I've said that the  crisis will bring about more social  insurance for the reasons I've outlined  and the consequence of that will be that  Americans become more desirous of  equality than they currently are I mean  there are let's face it there are about  1/2 of the population of the United  States there's not particularly like  more equality I mean this is probably a  fair characterization of the people who  voted for McCain and the presidential  election which was about half the  population so I think preferences have  to change and my hope is that the crisis  will have have that good effect what the  steady state will be I don't know I mean  how egalitarian will the United States  become will it ever look like Sweden  maybe in five hundred years Wow okay  very good thank you very much for  joining us today thank you for more  information about Professor Romer and  his work please visit our website at  Yale edu backslash Macmillan Report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

